epartment of Orthopaedic Surgery, Duke University School of Medicine, Durham, United States.\") , [Yihan Liao](/?term=Liao+Y&cauthor_id=35179487)  [2](#short-view-affiliation-2 \"Department of Orthopaedic Surgery, Duke University School of Medicine, Durham, United States.\")   [3](#short-view-affiliation-3 \"Department of Pharmacology and Cancer Biology, Duke University School of Medicine, Durham, United States.\") , [Yinshi Ren](/?term=Ren+Y&cauthor_id=35179487)  [2](#short-view-affiliation-2 \"Department of Orthopaedic Surgery, Duke University School of Medicine, Durham, United States.\") , [Anthony J Mirando](/?term=Mirando+AJ&cauthor_id=35179487)  [2](#short-view-affiliation-2 \"Department of Orthopaedic Surgery, Duke University School of Medicine, Durham, United States.\") , [Tuyet Nguyen](/?term=Nguyen+T&cauthor_id=35179487)  [4](#short-view-affiliation-4 \"Program of Developmental and Stem Cell Biology, Duke University School of Medicine, Durham, United States.\") , [Wendi Guo](/?term=Guo+W&cauthor_id=35179487)  [2](#short-view-affiliation-2 \"Department of Orthopaedic Surgery, Duke University School of Medicine, Durham, United States.\")   [3](#short-view-affiliation-3 \"Department of Pharmacology and Cancer Biology, Duke University School of Medicine, Durham, United States.\") , [Deepika Sharma](/?term=Sharma+D&cauthor_id=35179487)  [2](#short-view-affiliation-2 \"Department of Orthopaedic Surgery, Duke University School of Medicine, Durham, United States.\") , [Douglas Rouse](/?term=Rouse+D&cauthor_id=35179487)  [5](#short-view-affiliation-5 \"Division of Laboratory Animal Resources, Duke University School of Medicine, Durham, United States.\") , [Colleen Wu](/?term=Wu+C&cauthor_id=35179487)  [1](#short-view-affiliation-1 \"Department of Cell Biology, Duke University School of Medicine, Durham, United States.\")   [2](#short-view-affiliation-2 \"Department of Orthopaedic Surgery, Duke University School of Medicine, Durham, United States.\")   [3](#short-view-affiliation-3 \"Department of Pharmacology and Cancer Biology, Duke University School of Medicine, Durham, United States.\") , [Kathryn Song Eng Cheah](/?term=Cheah+KSE&cauthor_id=35179487)  [6](#short-view-affiliation-6 \"School of Biomedical Sciences, University of Hong Kong, Hong Kong, Hong Kong.\") , [Courtney M Karner](/?term=Karner+CM&cauthor_id=35179487)  [1](#short-view-affiliation-1 \"Department of Cell Biology, Duke University School of Medicine, Durham, United States.\")   [2](#short-view-affiliation-2 \"Department of Orthopaedic Surgery, Duke University School of Medicine, Durham, United States.\") , [Matthew J Hilton](/?term=Hilton+MJ&cauthor_id=35179487)  [1](#short-view-affiliation-1 \"Department of Cell Biology, Duke University School of Medicine, Durham, United States.\")   [2](#short-view-affiliation-2 \"Department of Orthopaedic Surgery, Duke University School of Medicine, Durham, United States.\")\n\n### Affiliations\n\n-   1 Department of Cell Biology, Duke University School of Medicine, Durham, United States.\n-   2 Department of Orthopaedic Surgery, Duke University School of Medicine, Durham, United States.\n-   3 Department of Pharmacology and Cancer Biology, Duke University School of Medicine, Durham, United States.\n-   4 Program of Developmental and Stem Cell Biology, Duke University School of Medicine, Durham, United States.\n-   5 Division of Laboratory Animal Resources, Duke University School of Medicine, Durham, United States.\n-   6 School of Biomedical Sciences, University of Hong Kong, Hong Kong, Hong Kong.\n\n-   PMID: **35179487**\n-   PMCID: [PMC8893718](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8893718/)\n-   DOI: [10.7554/eLife.76932](https://doi.org/10.7554/elife.76932)\n\nItem in Clipboard\n\nFull text links Cite\n\nDisplay options\n\nDisplay options\n\nFormat AbstractPubMedPMID\n\n## Abstract\n\nHypertrophic chondrocytes give rise to osteoblasts during skeletal development; however, the process by which these non-mitotic cells make this transition is not well understood. Prior studies have also suggested that skeletal stem and progenitor cells (SSPCs) localize to the surrounding periosteum and serve as a major source of marrow-associated SSPCs, osteoblasts, osteocytes, and adipocytes during skeletal development. To further understand the cell transition process by which hypertrophic chondrocytes contribute to osteoblasts or other marrow associated cells, we utilized inducible and constitutive hypertrophic chondrocyte lineage tracing and reporter mouse models (*Col10a1CreERT2; Rosa26fs-tdTomato* and *Col10a1Cre; Rosa26fs-tdTomato*) in combination with a *PDGFRaH2B-GFP* transgenic line, single-cell RNA-sequencing, bulk RNA-sequencing, immunofluorescence staining, and cell transplantation assays. Our data demonstrate that hypertrophic chondrocytes undergo a process of dedifferentiation to generate marrow-associated SSPCs that serve as a primary source of osteoblasts during skeletal development. These hypertrophic chondrocyte-derived SSPCs commit to a CXCL12-abundant reticular (CAR) cell phenotype during skeletal development and demonstrate unique abilities to recruit vasculature and promote bone marrow establishment, while also contributing to the adipogenic lineage.\n\n**Keywords:** adipocytes; bone marrow; developmental biology; hypertrophic chondrocytes; mouse; osteoblasts; progenitor cells; skeletal stem.\n\n© 2022, Long et al.\n\n[PubMed Disclaimer](/disclaimer/)\n\n## Conflict of interest statement\n\nJL, AL, YL, YR, AM, TN, WG, DS, DR, CW, CK, MH No competing interests declared, KC Reviewing editor, eLife\n\n## Figures\n\n [![Figure 1.](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/3299/8893718/2e4033582171/elife-76932-fig1.gif)](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/3299/8893718/d76dafe7d56b/elife-76932-fig1.jpg) \n\n**\n\nFigure 1.. Hypertrophic chondrocytes are the primary…\n\n**\n\n**\n\nFigure 1.. Hypertrophic chondrocytes are the primary source of osteoblasts/osteocytes in trabec